{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-48580",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & =g(1) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({b}^{2}+2xb) \\\\  & ={b}^{2}+2(1)b \\\\  & ={b}^{2}+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ -1 & ={b}^{2}+2b \\\\ {b}^{2}+2b+1 & =0 \\\\ (b+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{(b+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |b+1| & =0 \\\\ b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$ for $b=-1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
